17] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d0e8ef1-dbd3-5d5d-b9cb-f3092550c8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-5)(2-6)+(6-5)(4-6)+(8-5)(10-6)+(2-5)(8-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-4)+(1)(-2)+(3)(4)+(-3)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4-2+12-6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{-1,\\,6,\\,2,\\,5\\},\\,y=\\{-2,\\,5,\\,7,\\,6\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=3$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cf798d3-99fb-51d5-934b-1e9180edf066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268973 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1-3)(-2-4)+(6-3)(5-4)+(2-3)(7-4)+(5-3)(6-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4)(-6)+(3)(1)+(-1)(3)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[24+3-3+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{1,\\,4,\\,7,\\,12\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,4,\\,2,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a837e578-9d21-592b-8de3-2aa17ce381f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268976 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-6)(2-4)+(4-6)(4-4)+(7-6)(2-4)+(12-6)(8-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-2)+(-2)(0)+(1)(-2)+(6)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[10+0-2+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,-2,\\,5,\\,1\\},\\,y=\\{-7,\\,5,\\,4,\\,-2\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8776d2b8-cc6d-5825-8f19-b2c66c611b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268979 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+(-2)+5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-7)+5+4+(-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-7-0)+(-2-2)(5-0)+(5-2)(4-0)+(1-2)(-2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-7)+(-4)(5)+(3)(4)+(-1)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-14-20+12+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Time Spent Practicing, \nin hours ($x$)** | $4$ | $5$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Points Scored ($y$)** | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows Mary's time practicing her jump shot each week and the number of points she scores in the Friday varsity basketball game. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a871439-561a-57b5-a2c7-bd992bf74497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268981 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-6)(4-10)+(5-6)(8-10)+(7-6)(12-10)+(8-6)(16-10)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-6)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(2)(6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+2+2+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe Size (USA) ($y$)** | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nMr. Damon recorded the age and shoe size of four tennis players at his academy. The table above shows the information he collected. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd4f8095-844e-57fc-88d9-718d41a408a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268991 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(7-10)(7-8)+(9-10)(8-8)+(11-10)(9-8)+(13-10)(8-8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-1)+(-1)(0)+(1)(1)+(3)(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[3+0+1+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1071 "$(3+x)^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cf853ca-6a16-5936-9604-4bf59d63715b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269094 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3+x)^{4},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=x,$ $n=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3+x)^{4}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(3)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(3)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{4}+4{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(3)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =81+108x+54{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1071 "${(2x-y)}^{5}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1f57d7b-a958-55ab-a552-4e907ee5b53e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269098 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2x-y)}^{5},$ we have  $a=2x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(2x-y)}^{5}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-y)^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(2x)^{5}(-y)^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(2x)^{5-1}(-y)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(2x)^{5-2}(-y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(2x)^{5}+5(2x)^{4}(-y)+10(2x)^{3}(-y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1071 "${(x-y)}^{8}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bfb4f9e-e9db-5bd6-a1f7-0c71987ddffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269100 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-y)}^{8},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=8.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-y)}^{8}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-y)}^{8} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0}){(x)}^{8}{(-y)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(x)}^{8-1}{(-y)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(x)}^{8-2}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{8}+8{(x)}^{7}{(-y)}^{1}+28{(x)}^{6}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $A$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${A}^{T}+A$ is lower triangular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccdf19a6-45a3-5b56-b00e-b00d25e501ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269146 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries below the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries above the main diagonal equal to zero: \n\n$$\n{A}^{T}+A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/