186045417] [17592186271807 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271807 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a40675b2-41a5-5c75-8425-9a3a29305edf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6c/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$a$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$b$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$c$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf86f867-a1a5-56dc-89e8-e2255e71399b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271964 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(Y<2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cdcd4af-d654-55b5-9a81-3fe7c8ead4ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272361 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{4}{7}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<2) & =P(Y\\in \\{1\\}) \\\\  & =P(Y=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(|Y|>0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d18fe4f-c908-58f7-9a78-04f4bc6d6db6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>0) & =P(Y\\in \\{-1,1\\}) \\\\  & =P(Y=-1)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.2+0.45 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0f4e8ee-b7fc-5bf2-8157-bde25fb332d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272384 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in \\{2,4\\},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272499 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272499 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272499 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ca6964b-f001-50a6-962e-37568669ccd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1499dc15-38f5-5d95-918a-72ffb6809888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272617 #edb/ref 1080 "6.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ae360b9-cddd-560a-a2f3-3d0a0df3d66e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272641 #edb/ref 1080 "64"